<script src="{{ 'featured-collection-slider.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>

{% comment %}    Script code can be added immediately after the end schema      {% endcomment %}

{% unless section.settings.quick_add == 'none' %}
  {{ 'quick-add.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
  <script src="{{ 'product-form.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
  <script src="{{ 'quick-add.js' | asset_url }}" defer="defer"></script>
{% endunless %}





{% comment %}    Add this block right after the your script      {% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign products_to_display = section.settings.collection.all_products_count

  if section.settings.collection.all_products_count > section.settings.products_to_show
    assign products_to_display = section.settings.products_to_show
    assign more_in_collection = true
  endif
-%}





<div class="color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} featured-collection featured-collection-{{ section.id }}
    {% if section.settings.full_width %} 
     featured-collection--full-width
     {% endif %}">
    
    <div class="featured-collection__heading">

   
        <h2 class="inline-richtext {{ section.settings.heading_size }} 
          
          {% if settings.animations_reveal_on_scroll %} scroll-trigger animate--slide-in
           {% endif %}">
           {{ section.settings.heading }}</h2>


         {% if section.settings.show_description %}
            <div class="featured-collection__description {{ section.settings.description_style }}
              {% if settings.animations_reveal_on_scroll %} scroll-trigger animate--slide-in
           {% endif %}">
                {{ section.settings.collection.description }}
            </div>
        {% elsif section.settings.description != blank %}
            <div class="featured-collection__description {{ section.settings.description_style }}">
                {{ section.settings.description }}
            </div>
        {% endif %}










    </div>
    
    









    <featured-slider>
      <ul
        class="featured-collection__grid
      {% if section.settings.swipe_on_mobile %}
        featured-collection__grid--swipe
      {% endif %}
       {% if section.settings.enable_desktop_slider %}
      featured-collection__grid--slider-desktop
      {% endif %} "
        style="
      --columns-desktop: {{ section.settings.columns_desktop }};
      --columns-mobile: {{ section.settings.columns_mobile }}
        "
      >






      {% if section.settings.collection.products.size > 0 %}

    {% for product in section.settings.collection.products limit: section.settings.products_to_show %}
        <li class="featured-collection__item 
          {% if settings.animations_reveal_on_scroll %} scroll-trigger animate--slide-in
           {% endif %}"

           {% if settings.animations_reveal_on_scroll %}
        data-cascade
        style="--animation-order: {{ forloop.index }};"
    {% endif %}
          
          >
            {% render 'card-product',
               card_product: product,
                media_aspect_ratio: section.settings.image_ratio,
                image_shape: section.settings.image_shape,
                show_vendor: section.settings.show_vendor,
                show_rating: section.settings.show_rating,
                show_secondary_image: section.settings.show_secondary_image,
                quick_add: section.settings.quick_add
            %}
        </li>
    {% endfor %}

{% else %}

    {% for i in (1..section.settings.columns_desktop) %}
        <li class="featured-collection__item">
            {% render 'card-product',
                media_aspect_ratio: section.settings.image_ratio,
                image_shape: section.settings.image_shape,
                placeholder_image: 'product-apparel-1'
            %}
        </li>
    {% endfor %}

{% endif %}



{% comment %}

.products.size > 0 asks "does this collection actually have products?"
 It is false both when no collection is picked and when the picked collection is empty, 
 so one check covers both cases.
{% for i in (1..3) %} is a loop over a number range instead of a list. 
It runs 1, 2, 3, so you get as many placeholders as you have desktop columns. 
You never use i inside the loop here, which is fine. It's only there to repeat the block.
placeholder_image: 'product-apparel-1' is a parameter that card-product understands. 
With no card_product passed in, the snippet draws a built-in sample image instead. 
This is also a nice way to test your grid layout without needing real products.

{% endcomment %}










      </ul>


      {% comment %} slider button and counter  {% endcomment %}
      {% if section.settings.enable_desktop_slider %}
    <div class="featured-collection__slider-buttons">
        <button type="button" class="featured-collection__slider-btn featured-collection__slider-btn--prev">
            &larr;
        </button>

        <div class="featured-collection__slider-counter">
            <span class="featured-collection__counter-current">1</span>
            <span> / </span>
            <span class="featured-collection__counter-total">{{ products_to_display }}</span>
        </div>

        <button type="button" class="featured-collection__slider-btn featured-collection__slider-btn--next">
            &rarr;
        </button>
    </div>
{% endif %}





    </featured-slider>





  {% if section.settings.show_view_all_button and more_in_collection %}
<div class="featured-collection__view-all {% if settings.animations_reveal_on_scroll %} scroll-trigger animate--slide-in
           {% endif %}">
    <a href="{{ section.settings.collection.url }}" class="featured-collection__view-all-btn
            featured-collection__view-all-btn--{{ section.settings.view_all_style }}">
                    View all
                </a>
</div>

 {% endif %}






    
</div>



<style>

 .featured-collection-{{ section.id }} {
    padding-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
    padding-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
  }

  /* section.id is unique per section instance. 
  If you drag this section onto the page twice, each copy gets its own section.id 
  (something like template--123__featured_collection), 
  so .featured-collection-{{ section.id }} produces a different CSS class for each one. 
  That's what lets two copies of the same section have different padding values without fighting each other.

  */
  
.featured-collection--full-width {
    padding-left: 0;
    padding-right: 0;
}

.featured-collection__heading {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    font-size: 40px;
  }

.featured-collection__description {
    font-size: 16px;
    margin-top: 10px;
}

  .featured-collection__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--columns-mobile), 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .featured-collection__grid--swipe {
     grid-template-columns: none;
    grid-auto-flow: column;
    grid-auto-columns: calc((100% - (var(--columns-mobile) - 1) * 20px) / var(--columns-mobile));
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  }

  .featured-collection__grid--swipe .featured-collection__item {
    scroll-snap-align: start;

}

  @media screen and (min-width: 768px) {

    .featured-collection__grid {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--columns-desktop), 1fr);
    }


     .featured-collection__grid--slider-desktop {
        grid-template-columns: none;   /* <-- add this line */
        grid-auto-flow: column;
        grid-auto-columns: calc((100% - (var(--columns-desktop) - 1) * 20px) / var(--columns-desktop));
        /* This math formula automatically calculates the exact width of a single column when using CSS Grid.
        It ensures that no matter how many columns you want (var(--columns-desktop)), 
        they will all fit perfectly onto one single row, leaving exactly 20px of space (gaps) between them, 
        without stretching past 100% of the screen width. 


        If you have 4 columns, you only have 3 gaps between them.
        The math does: (4 - 1) * 20px → 3 * 20px = 60px.
        Result: The total space needed just for the gaps is 60px.

        Subtract the Gaps from the Total Width
        100% - (60px)


        The browser takes the full width available (100%) and 
        subtracts the gap space (60px).
        Result: You are left with the exact remaining space available only for the actual content blocks.
        

        (Remaining Space) / var(--columns-desktop)

        Finally, the browser takes that remaining space and 
        divides it cleanly by the number of columns (4).
        Result: Every column gets the exact same pixel width down to the decimal point.


        Why Use grid-auto-columns with this Math?
        Normally, CSS Grid wraps items to the next line. 
        However, in Shopify sections (like product carousels or sliders), 
        developers use grid-auto-columns to force items to stay on one single horizontal line 
        so users can swipe through them.
        By using this specific formula, the browser ensures that exactly 4 items fit perfectly on the screen at once, 
        and the 5th item sits just off-screen, waiting to be scrolled into view!

        */
        overflow-x: auto;
        scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;

        /* scroll-snap-type: x mandatory        tells the browser "when the user stops scrolling, 
        snap to a stopping point," and 
        scroll-snap-align: start         on each item tells it "this element is a valid stopping point.
        " No JavaScript needed for basic snap-scrolling — that's the whole trick. */

        padding-bottom: 10px;
    }


     .featured-collection__grid--slider-desktop .featured-collection__item {
        scroll-snap-align: start;
    }



    
  }

.featured-collection__view-all {
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: 30px;
}

.featured-collection__view-all-btn--link {
    text-decoration: underline;
}

.featured-collection__view-all-btn--outline {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 10px 24px;
    border: 1px solid black;
}

.featured-collection__view-all-btn--solid {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 10px 24px;
    background: black;
    color: white;
}




.featured-collection__slider-buttons {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 10px;
    margin-top: 20px;
}

.featured-collection__slider-btn {
    width: 40px;
    height: 40px;
    border: 1px solid black;
    background: white;
    cursor: pointer;
}




counter number styling

.featured-collection__slider-buttons {
    align-items: center;
}

.featured-collection__slider-counter {
    min-width: 50px;
    text-align: center;
}







to customize the scroll bar 

.featured-collection__grid--slider-desktop::-webkit-scrollbar,
.featured-collection__grid--swipe::-webkit-scrollbar {
    height: 8px;
}

.featured-collection__grid--slider-desktop::-webkit-scrollbar-track,
.featured-collection__grid--swipe::-webkit-scrollbar-track {
    background: #eeeeee;
    border-radius: 10px;
}

.featured-collection__grid--slider-desktop::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb,
.featured-collection__grid--swipe::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb {
    background: #333333;
    border-radius: 10px;
}



</style>

{% comment %}
  Yes, using Dawn's global `slider-component` is a good choice for now. 
  It's less code for you to write, 
  it's the pattern the reference uses, and it gives you the counter and the disabled-at-the-start buttons for free.

**Should you touch `global.js`? No, just read it.**
- Almost every section on your site depends on it. 
One typo can break the menu, cart, variant picker and more, all at once.
- Theme updates can overwrite it, so your edits would get lost.
- If you want different behavior, 
do what you just did (instead of using `slider-component` you used 'featured-slider' and create your
own custom JS file in the asset folder): 
write your own custom element in a separate file with a unique name.


**What's usually inside Dawn's `global.js`.
** I'm going from memory of Dawn, and versions differ, 
so use `Ctrl+F` on your own file to confirm each one.

1. **`slider-component`**: the horizontal sliders (like your featured collection), with prev/next buttons and the "1 / 4" counter.
2. **`slideshow-component`**: the big banner slideshow, with autoplay and pause.
3. **`quantity-input`**: the plus/minus buttons on quantity fields.
4. **`menu-drawer` and `header-drawer`**: the mobile hamburger menu that slides in.
5. **`modal-dialog` and `modal-opener`**: popups, like the ones that open when you click something.
6. **`deferred-media`**: videos and 3D models that only load when clicked.
7. **`variant-selects` and `variant-radios`**: the size/color pickers on product pages that update price and images.
8. **`product-recommendations`**: loads the "You may also like" products after the page loads.
9. **`bulk-add` and `bulk-modal`**: the bulk quantity features (related to `quick_add: bulk`).
10. **Helper functions, not tags**: `trapFocus`, `removeTrapFocus`, `debounce`, `fetchConfig` and `getFocusableElements`. These are used by the components above for keyboard accessibility and network requests.

Some things you might expect there are usually in their own files:
`cart.js`, `product-form.js`, `quick-add.js`, `predictive-search.js` and `animations.js`.

**What to read in it.
** Find the `SliderComponent` class and note what it searches for. 
In Dawn it's roughly:
- the list: `[id^="Slider-"]`
- the slides: `[id^="Slide-"]`
- the buttons: `button[name="previous"]` and `button[name="next"]`
- the counter: `.slider-counter--current` and `.slider-counter--total`

Those are the labels your HTML must use for the global slider to work. 
If they match what you see, we can convert your section to use it. 




global.js runs on every page automatically,
So Loading it again using script would cause a bug.

If your section also included global.js, 
the browser would try to run it a second time and 
register every custom element twice. 
You'd get errors like 'slider-component' has already been used with this registry, 
and other parts of the site (menu, cart, variant picker) could misbehave.

What you do need to load in your section is the slider's CSS. 
In the reference, that's this line at the top:

liquid
{{ 'component-slider.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

The global slider's layout styles live there, and global.js doesn't include them.



So if you convert your section to use the global slider, you would:

Delete your <script src="...featured-slider.js..."> line
Add the component-slider.css line
Change your HTML labels to match what SliderComponent looks for 
(Slider- ids, Slide- ids, previous/next button names)
Remove your own slider CSS and JS






{% endcomment %}